You are on **Linux**.  The DEFAULT shell is **bash** — use standard Unix
commands.
File paths use forward slashes (`/`).
The `terminal` parameter is OPTIONAL: omit it (or pass `"auto"`) and the best
available shell is chosen for you (bash -> zsh -> sh).  Pass an explicit
`terminal` (`zsh`, `sh`, `python`, ...) only when a script genuinely needs
that interpreter — the exception, not the default.  Each explicit
(terminal, cwd) pair keeps a persistent session, so cwd and exported vars
survive across calls with the same pair.
A running command shows output live; the user may stop it (you get the partial
output plus `interrupted: true`).  If a command exceeds its `timeout` its shell
is terminated and the result is flagged `timed_out: true`.  Trust `exit_code`
in the result JSON to decide success — never assume 0.
